      "problem": "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-10x=-21.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-10x+25$ is a perfect square.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-10x+25=(x-5{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-21 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}-25 & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $25$ to both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2}-25+25 & =-21+25 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x-5| & =2 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}={3}^{2}+{7}^{2}=58.\n$$",
